  "query_preview": "..\nTonight-Friday: A baggy trough remains over the region this \nevening. One more embedded shortwave rounding the trough will \nusher a weak front through the region delivering mid/high clouds\nand small chance for sprinkles (5-15%). A transition toward \nzonal flow returns on Friday. This will allow for a subtle \nwarming trend with temperatures rebounding back into the 80s. \nWinds on Friday will increase relative to Thursday with speeds \nnear 10 mph and gusts of 15 to 20 mph. Locally stronger winds \nwill impact the Cascade Gaps each evening given the persistent \nonshore flow and alignment of diurnals and pressure gradients. \n\nIndependence Day: A deeper trough swings into southern BC Saturday \nresulting in a tightening pressure gradient across the Inland \nNW. Saturday will become breezy to locally windy region-wide \nresulting in elevated to critical fire weather conditions. With \nthe cooler air remaining to the north and increasing downsloping\nflow off the Cascades, temperatures will continue to warm \nSaturday reaching the mid 80s to lower 90s. A stronger dry slot \nwill pivot into the region Saturday afternoon into the night. \nThis will result in significant drying with PWAT anomalies \nfalling as low as 40-50% of normal and relative humidity values \nin the teens to twenties. The strongest winds are expected to \nimpact the foothills of the Cascades, Waterville Plateau, \nsouthern Okanogan Valley, and Colville Reservation. Fire Weather\nWatches have been issued to account for this combination of \nwinds and low humidities. We will need to examine trends for \nnearby zones such as the Methow Valley and Moses Lake Area to \nsee if watches need to be expanded. The foothills of the Blue \nMountains along the Hwy 12 corridor is another area that will \nexperience a push of strong winds though these stronger winds \nseem to be shorter lived. For the remainder of the Idaho \nPanhandle, Northeastern WA, Spokane Area, Palouse, and southern \nColumbia Basin there will be a noticeable increase in winds but \nspeeds look to remain below critical thresholds. Needless to \nsay, elevated fire weather conditions will be in place. Please \nuse caution around dry fuels as fires could spread rapidly. \nWinds will diminish across North Idaho, eastern Basin, and \nNortheastern WA Saturday evening. This may not be the case for \nthe East Slopes, Western Basin, and Okanogan Valley the backside\nof the aforementioned trough swings inland overnight. These \nareas will also be coping with increasing dryness levels with \npoor overnight recoveries of 40-50%. \n\nSunday-Thursday: Residual breeziness remains on Sunday as the \ntrough is slowly departing to the northeast. Conditions will \nremain warm and very dry. Winds will not be as gusty as Saturday\nbut still elevated, especially in the morning and early \nafternoon. High pressure strengthens over the region Monday and \nTuesday pushing temperatures in the 90s region-wide with \nmoderate heat risk for most lowlands. Focus for the next \nimpactful weather pattern comes later Tuesday into Wednesday as \nyet another trough swings toward the PacNW and breaks down the \nheat. This will be our next concern for fire weather conditions \nwith the passage of a dry cold front. Models are still sorting \nout the details for timing and strength of this incoming system \nbut given the anticipated pressure gradient and signature of \ncold air advection with this system, it has strong potential to \nproduce winds stronger than Saturday and critical fire weather \nconditions to the Inland NW. /sb",
